findings.fix:
Validate the index at the point of use, and make CI enforce it. In `rawUrl`: reject any path segment that is not a plain filename, and constrain the pinned fields:

```ts
const SEG = /^[A-Za-z0-9._ ()+-]+$/;   // no empty, no '.', no '..'
const OWNER = /^[A-Za-z0-9-]{1,39}$/;
const REPO  = /^[A-Za-z0-9._-]{1,100}$/;
const SHA   = /^[0-9a-f]{40}$/;

export function rawUrl(index: BaselineIndex, path: string): string {
  if (!OWNER.test(index.owner) || !REPO.test(index.repo) || !SHA.test(index.commit))
    throw new Error('baseline index is not pinned to a repo and full commit sha');
  const segs = path.split('/');
  if (!segs.every((s) => s !== '.' && s !== '..' && SEG.test(s)))
    throw new Error(`baseline index path is not a plain repo path: ${path}`);
  return `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/${index.owner}/${index.repo}/${index.commit}/${segs.map(encodeURIComponent).join('/')}`;
}
```

Requiring a full 40-hex SHA also stops a branch name (`main`) being substituted for the pin. Then add a test that reads every `baselines/*.index.json` and asserts `rawUrl(index, p)` for each `p` yields a URL whose pathname still starts with `/${owner}/${repo}/${commit}/` — that is the assertion `src/network.test.ts` is missing, because it only checks hostnames appearing literally in source.

